.TH vellum "" "Command"
.SH NAME
vellum \- GUI technical drawing and schematic editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBvellum [\fIoptions\fB] [\fIfile\fB]\fR
.br
\fBvellum [\fIoptions\fB] +\fIx,y \fB[\fIfile\fB]\fR
.br
\fBvellum \-print\fR|\fB\-pic\fR|\fB\-net\fR|\fB\-hpgl\fR|\fB\-bom\fR|\fB\-check\fR|\fB\-ps\fR|\fB\-dxf\fR|\fB\-len\fR|\fB\-spice\fR \
[\fB\-wide\fR] [\fB\-fit\fR] [\fB\-scale\fR \fIn\fR] \fIfile.d ...\fR
.br
\fBvellum \-renum\fR [\fB\-base\fR \fIn\fR] \fIfile.d\fR > \fIout.d\fR
.br
\fBvellum \-diff\fR [\fB\-mark\fR] \fIold.d new.d\fR
.br
\fBvellum \-where\fR \fIstring file.d ...\fR
.br
\fBvellum \-symsheet\fR \fIlib.sym\fR > \fIcard.d\fR
.br
\fBvellum \-mksym\fR \fICODE\fR [\fB\-pfx\fR \fIp\fR] [\fB\-org\fR \fIx,y\fR] [\fB\-scale\fR \fIn\fR] [\fB\-type\fR \fINAME=t\fR] \fIfile.d\fR >> \fIlib.sym\fR
.br
\fBvellum \-symcheck\fR \fIlib.sym ...\fR
.br
\fBvellum \-book\fR \fIsheet.d ...\fR > \fIcontents.d\fR
.br
\fBvellum \-tile\fR [\fB\-n\fR] \fB\-print\fR|\fB\-ps\fR|\fB\-hpgl\fR \fIbig.d\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
vellum -- GUI technical drawing and schematic editor
Usage:	vellum [ options ] [ file ]
	vellum [ options ] +x,y [ file ]
	vellum -print|-pic|-net|-hpgl|-bom|-check|-ps|-dxf|-len|-spice
		[-wide] [-fit] [-scale n] file.d ...
	vellum -renum [-base n] file.d > out.d
	vellum -diff [-mark] old.d new.d
	vellum -where string file.d ...
	vellum -symsheet lib.sym > card.d
	vellum -mksym CODE [-pfx p] [-org x,y] [-scale n]
		[-type NAME=t] file.d >> lib.sym
	vellum -symcheck lib.sym ...
	vellum -book sheet.d ... > contents.d
	vellum -tile [-n] -print|-ps|-hpgl big.d
Keys:	r rotate, m mirror, d duplicate, n properties, x/Del delete,
	u undo, v zoom-to-fit, f to front, j/J group/ungroup,
	y make symbol from the selection,
	* / scale selection x2 / half,
	1-6 align L/R/T/B + distribute, g grid, < > sheets, Esc select,
	F2 save, F3 open, F4 new, F5-F7 cut/copy/paste, F8 settings
.HE
.B vellum
is the drawing board of this workstation: electronic schematics,
block diagrams, flowcharts, network maps, software structure charts
and figures for manuals, with clean printed output and full
membership in the Unix pipeline.
It must be run under the
.B zview
desktop and accepts the standard window
.I options
of GUI applications; see
.BR zview .
The optional
.I file
argument names a drawing to open.
.PP
The file name sits in its own bar at the top, with a `*' while the
drawing has unsaved changes; at the bar's right edge the
.B <
and
.B >
cells walk a numbered sheet set \- they sit on the file line because
the sheet is a property of the FILE (the same actions as the
.B <
and
.B >
keys; see SHEET SETS).
The TOOLBAR row of cells sits under it, and the palette down the
left edge holds the symbol bank.
Symbols come from LIBRARY FILES named in /usr/vellum/etc/libs:
.B discrete
(resistor through opamp),
.B logic
(gates and flip-flops),
.B flow
(flowchart stencils),
.B net
(network-map stencils),
.B arch
(software structure stencils),
.B pid
(process / P&ID stencils),
.B power
(one-line electrical) and
.B plan
(floor-plan stencils), plus the user scratch library
/usr/vellum/etc/symbols when it holds anything.
Every loaded symbol stays resolvable at once \- a drawing may mix
libraries \- but the palette shows ONE group at a time: the header
cell above the bank names it and clicking the header switches to the
next.
After the last library comes the
.B shapes
pseudo-group: the nine STRETCHABLE shapes (box, rounded box,
diamond, oval, parallelogram, drum, document, circle, ellipse), the
four CONNECTOR styles (hv, line, arrow, harrow), the Arc tool, the
Net name marker and the Dim (dimension) tool.
The toolbar's
.B Lib
cell lists the libraries on file in a scrollable dialog, and
.B Edit
opens the current library in the
.B symedit
symbol editor.
.PP
The tools:
.DS
.ta 8n 16n
	\fBSel\fR	click selects (a grouped object selects its whole
		group), drag moves; a drag on EMPTY canvas selects
		everything inside the rectangle.  A selected SHAPE
		shows corner handles \- dragging one resizes it \-
		and a selected line, wire, connector or dimension
		shows ENDPOINT grips (a polyline its vertices):
		dragging one re-routes and re-snaps the end
	\fBWire\fR	drag or click each end; wires route H-then-V, snap
		to pins and re-anchor so a run chains corner by
		corner \- \fBEsc\fR or a click back on the anchor
		ends the run
	\fBLine\fR	like Wire but straight \- and a chained run folds
		into ONE polyline object
	\fBBox\fR	drag draws a rectangle
	\fBCirc\fR	drag from the centre draws a circle
	\fBText\fR	click asks for a label and places it
	\fBDel\fR	click deletes what it hits
.DE
Between the tool groups the
.B Grid
cell toggles the grid dots; then the ACTION cells
.B Rot ,
.B Mir ,
.B Name
and
.B Dup
arm click-to-apply versions of the keys;
.B In
and
.B Out
zoom (also
.B +
and
.B \- ,
and
.B v
zooms to fit the whole drawing);
.B Fr
and
.B Bk
move the selection to the front or back of the stacking order; and
the
.B L\fIn\fR
cell cycles the ACTIVE layer new objects land on.
.PP
A SHAPE is placed by dragging its rectangle, then typing the label
(drawn centred, clipped to the shape); shapes expose ATTACHMENT
POINTS at their edge midpoints and corners.
A CONNECTOR is drawn with the wire gestures but snaps to attachment
points and symbol pins, and STAYS ATTACHED: moving or resizing the
target re-routes the connector.
The
.B arrow
and
.B harrow
styles carry an arrowhead at the far end.
The Arc cell draws a circular arc in two stages: drag from the
centre to the start point, then click where the arc ends.
The Net cell places a named net marker for the netlist export.
.PP
The
.B Dim
cell draws a DIMENSION point to point with the wire gestures; both
ends snap to attachment points, so dimensioning a shape's edge is
two clicks.
It lands on the annotation layer, with extension ticks, arrowheads
both ends and a centred label.
An EMPTY label is automatic: the measured distance times the sheet
unit, integer formatted (``85 mm''); any text set through the style
dialog overrides it, and emptying the text goes back to automatic.
The sheet unit (``one grid unit is 5 mm'') is set in Settings and
saved in the drawing; coordinates stay grid units \- only labels
multiply.
A LEADER needs no tool of its own: an
.B arrow
connector plus a text label already is one.
.PP
The
.B n
key (or the Name tool) edits a symbol's designator and value; on any
other object it opens the STYLE dialog: solid/dashed/dotted line
style, white/gray/black fill (fills stack in z-order), bold (width
2),
.B Hatch
(a gray fill renders as 45-degree hatching \- cross-section shading),
the object's layer, its text and \- for a text label \- one of
three sizes and
.B Vert ,
which turns the label 90 degrees (axis labels, vertical dimensions).
A text label may hold several lines separated by `|'.
A polyline checked
.B Smooth
renders as a spline through its points, and
.B \-pic
exports it as one.
A FILLED polyline is a closed even-odd fill over its points \- the
arbitrary solid shape mechanical sketches want \- on screen, in
every export and in the preview.
.SH LAYERS
Four fixed layers: 0
.B drawing
(the default), 1
.B annotation ,
2
.B frame
(title block, sheet border) and 3
.B construction ,
which never prints.
The Settings dialog (window menu) checks which layers are VISIBLE
and which PRINT, picks the snap pitch (1 or 2 units) and the sheet
preset (160x120, or A4/A3/A2 at 8 dots per unit \- now that pages
can leave on a laser or a plotter, the screenful is not the natural
page for every drawing), and its
.B Frame
button stamps the TEMPLATE /usr/vellum/etc/frame.d \- ordinary
drawing lines fed through the parser onto the frame layer, with
.BR $F " " $D " " $S " " $R
substituted in text values (file name, date, ``Sheet n/m'', revision)
\- so a company title block is an edit to that file, not a rebuild.
The sheet edge shows as a dotted construction guide.
.SH EDITING
.DS
.ta 6n 22n
	\fBr\fR	rotate a quarter turn (ANY selection turns about
		its centre; a lone symbol turns in place)
	\fBm\fR	mirror (likewise: any selection)
	\fBd\fR	duplicate, two units down-right
	\fBn\fR	properties (name/value or style dialog)
	\fBx\fR, \fBDel\fR	delete the selection
	\fBu\fR	UNDO the last commit (once more redoes)
	\fBa\fR	search again: the next hit for the pattern and
		direction the Search dialog last used
	\fBv\fR	zoom to fit the drawing
	\fBf\fR	selection to the front (stacking)
	\fBj\fR, \fBJ\fR	group / ungroup the selection
	\fB*\fR, \fB/\fR	scale the selection: double / halve about
		its corner (halving rounds odd coordinates
		and the status line says so)
	\fB1 2 3 4\fR	align left / right / top / bottom
	\fB5 6\fR	distribute evenly, horizontally / vertically
	\fBs w l b c t e\fR	arm Sel/Wire/Line/Box/Circ/Text/Del
	\fBg\fR	toggle the grid dots (also the Grid cell)
	\fB+\fR, \fB\-\fR	zoom in / out
	\fB<\fR, \fB>\fR	previous / next sheet of a numbered set
	\fBEsc\fR	cancel the armed tool, placement or run;
		a second \fBEsc\fR drops the selection
	\fBF2 F3 F4\fR	Save / Open / New (as in \fBzedit\fR)
	\fBF5 F6 F7\fR	Cut / Copy / Paste
	\fBF8\fR	the Settings dialog
	\fBF9\fR	the Search dialog
	\fBF11\fR	Help: this page, opened in \fBzman\fR
		(also the window menu's Help entry)
	arrows	pan; \fBClear/Home\fR pans to the origin
.DE
The window menu's
.B Print
entry opens the print dialog: scale, wide, and the three verbs \-
.B Print
spools the drawing through velxport into
.BR lpr ,
.B Preview
opens a window (velprev) drawing the printable extent through the
SAME renderer that feeds the printer, so what it shows is what the
paper gets.
The
.B Search
entry searches designators, values, text and net names for a
substring, selects the hit and pans it to centre.
.B Next
and
.B Prev
walk the object list either way round from the current selection,
wrapping; with nothing selected they open at the first and the last
object respectively.
Matching ignores case unless
.B "Match case"
is ticked.
The pattern, the direction and the box are remembered, so
.B a
on the canvas (or a second
.BR Search )
steps to the next hit without the dialog.
Its
.B Sheets
button lists the numbered set and jumps by number.
The
.B Dup
toolbar cell with a live selection opens the ARRAY dialog (columns,
rows, pitch) \- pin headers and terminal strips in one commit; the
armed Dup tool still copies single objects click by click.
While a wire, line, connector or dimension drag is up, the status
line's coordinate cell shows the run's LENGTH times the sheet unit \-
measuring without committing a dimension.
Zoom reaches down to 2 px per unit \- the whole A4 sheet in one
window \- where the grid thins to every fourth unit.
A text, shape label or dimension override may run to 39 characters;
the value of a symbol stays 15.
Cut, Copy and Paste (window menu) go through the system clipboard AS
TEXT in the drawing format \- paste a fragment into another Vellum,
or into
.B zedit
as text.
A middle CLICK pastes at the pointer (the selection, else the
clipboard); a middle DRAG pans the sheet.
Persistent groups select, move, copy and delete as one until
ungrouped.
When the drawing is modified, an autosave writes
.IB file .bak
every five minutes.
.PP
.BI vellum " +x,y file.d"
opens with the view CENTRED on that grid point and the object there
selected.
Every asking mode prints its hits as
.IR "file: ... at x,y" ,
so this hands a report straight back to the board:
.B "vellum -where TODO *.d"
stops being a report and becomes a work list.
.PP
The
.B y
key turns the SELECTION into a library stencil \- the editor's face of
.BR "vellum -mksym" .
It asks for the stencil's code, its designator prefix, the library file
to append to, and the scale, then writes the selection out as an
ordinary drawing and runs the exporter on it, so a stencil made here
and one made from make(1) are the same bytes.
The
.B N
net-name markers in the selection become the PINS, in z-order; pin
types are set afterwards in
.BR symedit ,
because a type is a property of the library and a guessed one builds a
wrong ERC.
A running
.B vellum
reads its libraries only at start-up, so the new stencil appears in the
palette next time.

.SH "SHEET SETS"
A sheet is a file; a drawing that spans sheets is a SET of numbered
files
.RI ( amp1.d ,
.IR amp2.d ,
\&...).
The
.B >
key saves the current sheet and loads the next \- past the last one
it offers to create it, which is how a set grows \- and
.B <
walks back.
The Frame stamp adds ``Sheet n/m'' when the file name carries a
number.
Shell globs are the set syntax for the exporters:
.B "vellum \-net amp?.d"
computes nets per sheet and MERGES them BY NAME \- a net named (by an
N marker or GND/VCC) VBUS on sheet 1 is the same conductor as VBUS on
sheet 3; unnamed nets stay sheet-local and print qualified
(NET\-2.3 = sheet 2, net 3).
.B \-print
emits the form feed between sheets, and
.B \-bom
yields ONE parts list for the whole set.
An off-page reference is the OFFP stencil with its value reading
``\(->3'' and an N net name on its pin \- the netlist merge makes it
electrically true.
.PP
A BUS is an idiom, not an object: draw the trunk as a BOLD wire \-
graphics, not electricity \- and tap it with short wires carrying
.B "N D0"
\&...
.BR "N D7" ;
same-named taps merge across the whole sheet set, which is what a
bus MEANS.
.B \-check
flags a net name appearing on fewer than two pins in the set \- the
typo'd bit (D3 on one end, D8 on the other) that eyes cannot see.
.SH "HEADLESS EXPORT"
The export modes run WITHOUT a window \- from make(1), from
scripts, from a serial terminal.
.B vellum
hands any lowercase
.BI - mode
straight to the helper /usr/vellum/lib/velxport, which carries the
model, the format and every backend and links no graphics at all.
On a machine with NO BITMAP CARD, run that helper directly:
.B vellum
itself is a graphics client and cannot exec where the shared graphics
library is not loaded, even to pass its arguments on.  That is why the
shipped Makefile skeletons say
.BR V=/usr/vellum/lib/velxport .
Each mode takes ONE OR MORE sheet files:
.DS
.ta 4n 28n
	\fBvellum -print\fR f.d	Epson raster on stdout (ESC * bands,
		1 grid unit = 8 dots; pipe to \fBlpr\fR).
		\fB-wide\fR prints landscape (the page turned a
		quarter), \fB-scale\fR \fIn\fR picks n dots per
		grid unit \- small drawings print big
	\fBvellum -pic\fR f.d	troff pic source on stdout (16 units
		per inch), for figures in manuals; smooth
		polylines come out as pic splines
	\fBvellum -hpgl\fR f.d	HPGL pen-plotter commands on stdout;
		the annotation layer draws in the SECOND
		pen (SP2) \- the cheapest two-color print
		this machine will ever do
	\fBvellum -ps\fR f.d ...	PostScript (Level 1) on stdout, one
		page per sheet \- the laser's page.  Text
		is Courier sized so the monospace advance
		matches the editor cell: nothing collides
		on paper that did not collide on screen.
		Smooth polylines emit real curveto,
		vertical text rotates, arcs are true arcs.
		\fB-fit\fR scales the drawing to fill the
		page (for \fB-print\fR it picks the widest
		step of the discrete 2..32 ladder instead)
	\fBvellum -dxf\fR f.d ...	DXF (R10 entities) on stdout, for
		the office CAD: coordinates in grid units,
		arcs as true ARC entities, our layer
		number as the DXF layer name.  \fBveldxf\fR
		converts the other way, and the pair
		fix-points
	\fBvellum -renum\fR f.d	canonical designators: symbols
		sorted by (y, x), numbered per prefix from
		\fB-base\fR \fIn\fR (default 1), the drawing
		re-emitted on stdout.  One sheet per run; a
		set renumbers sheet by sheet with -base
		carrying the count across
	\fBvellum -net\fR f.d ...	netlist: one line per net, R1.2-style
		pins (named pins print Q1.B), GND/VCC and
		N markers name nets \- MERGED by name across
		the sheet set; designator-less stencils
		(rails, taps, off-page markers) carry nets
		but are never listed; warnings for single-
		pin nets and unconnected pins on stderr
	\fBvellum -bom\fR f.d ...	bill of materials over the whole
		set: count, stencil, value and the
		designators, sorted
	\fBvellum -check\fR f.d ...	prove the set: duplicate
		designators, dangling wire ends, parts with
		no value, symbol codes no library defines
		(lines the loader would DROP), sheets whose
		U units disagree, single-pin nets and
		unconnected pins \- plus the ERC: between
		TYPED pins (see \fBsymedit\fR), two outputs
		on a net conflict, an output must not drive
		a power rail, and a net whose typed pins
		are all inputs is undriven.  An untyped
		library produces silence, not noise.  The
		conductor checks
		(dangling ends, nets, pins) apply only to
		sheets that contain WIRES \- a diagram
		sheet's stencil pins are attachment
		points, not terminals.  One finding per
		line on stdout; the EXIT STATUS is the
		finding count, so make(1) gates on it:
		  check: ; vellum -check amp?.d
		  print: check ; vellum -print amp?.d | lpr
	\fBvellum -tile\fR f.d	the sheet BIGGER than the paper: the
		chosen backend runs once per PAGE with the
		drawing origin stepped, so a drawing wider
		than the head comes out as pages that TAPE
		TOGETHER \- one grid unit of overlap on each
		seam, crop marks at the corners and a
		"2/6 row B col 2" label in the margin, row-
		major so the pile is in reading order.
		Applies to \fB-print\fR, \fB-ps\fR and \fB-hpgl\fR, and composes
		with \fB-wide\fR and \fB-scale\fR.  It does NOT compose
		with \fB-fit\fR, which means "make this ONE
		page" and is the opposite instruction: that
		combination is an error, not a precedence
		rule.  \fB-n\fR prints how many pages and
		stops.  Without \fB-tile\fR, \fB-print\fR still clips at
		the head's 960 dots \- but says so on stderr
.DE
Layers marked no-print and the construction layer are skipped.
The file name
.B \-
is standard input, so the modes compose with each other:
.B "vellum -symsheet pid.sym | vellum -ps -fit -" .
.SH "ASKING THE SET"
A drawing set lives for years, and every question the shop asks about
it \- what changed, what it takes, where anything is, and what
everything means \- the set answers itself.
These modes are the shell's, not the window's; their natural asker is
make(1).
.DS
.ta 4n 28n
	\fBvellum -diff\fR \fIold new\fR	what CHANGED between two
		revisions. Matching is by IDENTITY, not by
		line: diff(1) on two .d files drowns in
		reordering, because the line order is the
		z-order, not the content. Symbols match by
		DESIGNATOR first (R7 is R7 wherever it
		moved in the list), everything else by
		exact (type, geometry, value) equality \- no
		fuzzy matching and no tolerance: an object
		that moved one unit is a remove plus an
		add, and that is the honest answer on a
		grid. An object still standing exactly
		where it stood, saying something else,
		reports as CHANGED. One line each, the
		-check shape:
		  new.d: R7 moved 40,20 -> 40,28
		  new.d: R9 added (R 47k)
		  new.d: C2 removed (C 100n)
		  new.d: T "Supply, sheet 1" changed
		The EXIT STATUS is the change count (capped
		254), so a Makefile gates a release print
		on "no drift since the approved revision"
		exactly the way it gates on -check
	\fBvellum -diff -mark\fR \fIo n\fR	... the answer AS A DRAWING on
		stdout: everything unchanged on its own
		layers, ADDED objects bold, REMOVED objects
		dashed on the annotation layer. No new
		viewer and no new UI \- the markup is
		ordinary objects, so \fBvellum -pic\fR draws it,
		\fB-print\fR prints it and the editor opens it
	\fBvellum -len\fR f.d ...	the TAKEOFF: wires, connectors and
		polylines measured in sheet units, per NET
		where the conductors have names and per
		LAYER otherwise, with a set total. H-V
		routes measure both legs, diagonals measure
		an integer square root. A closed FILLED
		polyline reports AREA instead (integer
		shoelace, halved with the odd half shown as
		".5"): sheet metal is ordered by area the
		way cable is ordered by length. A set whose
		sheets disagree about the unit is refused
		in one line rather than adding millimetres
		to inches
	\fBvellum -where\fR \fIstr\fR f.d	the set-wide Find: a case-blind
		substring over designators, values, text
		and net names \- the editor's Find contract
		re-stated over FILES \- one line per hit
		with the grid position, EXIT STATUS the hit
		count. Zero hits exits 0 silently, so the
		INVERTED use is free: \fBvellum -where TODO
		*.d\fR in a check: rule fails the build while
		a sheet still carries a TODO note
	\fBvellum -spice\fR f.d ...	a SPICE 2G6 deck on stdout: the
		same nets as \fB-net\fR, numbered (0 for the
		GND-named net), one card per designated
		R/C/L/D/Q, values parsed from the value
		field ("4k7", "100n", "2.2M" \- the suffix
		letter doubles as the decimal point, and
		the schematic's M emits as MEG). A D or Q
		card ends with its stencil CODE as the
		model name unless its value names one; pin
		ORDER is the library's, except that a
		library which NAMES its pins B/C/E (or A/K)
		orders the card the way SPICE wants \- the
		stock \fBdiscrete\fR library does. Everything the
		subset cannot say is SKIPPED AND COUNTED on
		stderr ("dropped: 1 OPA, 2 J"): a silent
		partial netlist is how simulations lie.
		Models are yours by design \- the deck ends
		.END and a wrapper file .INCLUDEs it beside
		the .MODEL lines
	\fBvellum -symsheet\fR \fIl.sym\fR	the library ON PAPER: a grid of
		every stencil in ONE .sym file with its
		code, designator prefix and pin types,
		emitted as an ordinary drawing. It is .d
		out, so it prints on any of the backends
		and the shop's binder fills itself from
		make:
		  vellum -symsheet pid.sym |
		    vellum -ps -fit -
		The card names its stencils by CODE like
		any drawing, so whatever reads it back must
		have that library loaded: put a library of
		your own in /usr/vellum/etc/libs first
.DE
.SH "LIBRARIES AS BUILD PRODUCTS"
A stencil is a drawing, and a library is a build product.
.PP
.B "vellum -mksym"
.I CODE
promotes an ordinary drawing into one
.B "symbol ... end"
block on stdout, so a shop's own standards get made on the board
instead of hand-cut in
.B symedit
at quarter-grid coordinates.
.B L
and
.B W
become
.B s
segments, a
.B B
box its four,
a
.B P
polyline its n\-1,
.B C
a circle,
.B A
an arc angles and all, and a
.B T
whose text is ONE CHARACTER a
.B t
glyph.
Everything else \- shapes, connectors, dimensions, nested symbols and
multi-character text \- is SKIPPED AND COUNTED on stderr, because a
silently half-converted stencil is a part that draws wrong forever.
.PP
The PINS come from the
.B N
net-name markers, in the order they appear in the file (which is the
z-order, and which
.B -spice
reads when a library does not name its pins); the net name becomes the
pin name, and an
.B N
called
.B \-
is a pin with no name.
Pin TYPES come from
.BI -type " NAME=t"
on the command line and never from the drawing: a type is a property of
the LIBRARY, and a guessed type builds a wrong ERC.
.PP
Coordinates are exact by construction.
A stencil is in quarter-grid units and a drawing in whole ones, so the
conversion is x4 and every pin lands on a multiple of 4 \- which is
what the loader wants.
.BI -scale " n"
divides instead, for a stencil sketched n times oversize so its detail
was drawable at all; at
.B "-scale 4"
a drawing unit IS a quarter unit and a hand-cut stencil's detail is
available.
.BI -org " x,y"
names the grid point that becomes (0,0); the default is the FIRST PIN,
because a stencil's origin is where it snaps (a sketch with no
.B N
markers at all falls back to the top-left of its printable extent, and
is told on stderr that it has no pins).
.PP
.B "vellum -symcheck"
proves the libraries the way
.B -check
proves the drawings, and it matters more: a wrong stencil is wrong in
EVERY drawing.
One finding per line as
.IR lib.sym ": " message ,
the exit status the finding count capped at 254 \- the
.B -check
shape exactly, so make(1) gates on it the same way.
It reports a code defined twice, a code defined in two of the libraries
given, a symbol with no geometry, a designator prefix with no pins, a
pin off a whole unit, a pin name that repeats, a pin left untyped in a
library that types the others, and \- the two that nothing else can say
\- pins past the eight the loader keeps, and symbols the loader DROPPED
because a pool filled.
Both of those the loader has always done silently, and the load order
then decides which stencil a drawing gets.
.PP
Together with make(1) that changes what a library IS, and costs no code
at all:
.DS
.ta 8n
	STENCILS = pump.d valve.d tank.d
	pid.sym: $(STENCILS)
		rm -f $@
		$(V) -mksym PUMP -pfx P pump.d >> $@
		$(V) -mksym VALVE -pfx V valve.d >> $@
		$(V) -mksym TANK -pfx TK tank.d >> $@
		$(V) -symcheck $@
	card: pid.sym ; $(V) -symsheet pid.sym | $(V) -ps -fit -
.DE
A stencil SKETCH is an ordinary drawing: it opens in the editor,
prints,
.BR -pic s
into the manual, and takes the whole revision workflow above
unchanged \- so the shop's stencils get revision control because they
ARE drawings.
Adding a stencil is adding a line; deleting one is deleting a file;
there is no library format to learn and no state anywhere but files.
/usr/vellum/etc/library.mk is the skeleton, and /usr/vellum/eg/sk holds
the nine sketches that build the stock
.B pid
library \- byte for byte, which is also the converter's fix point.
.PP
The same conversion has an interactive face:
.B symedit
grows
.B "Import drawing"
(the
.B i
key), which brings a drawing into the edit buffer under exactly these
rules so the promote-then-touch-up workflow is one program, and
.B Check
(the
.B k
key), which shows these findings for the open library.
.SH "THE SET AS A DOCUMENT"
.B "vellum -book"
emits an ORDINARY DRAWING listing the sheets given \- number, file
name, and the sheet's own title, which it takes from the first text
object on the FRAME layer (where the frame stamp puts
.BR $F ,
so a stamped set titles itself), falling back to the largest text on
the sheet and then to the file name.
It is
.B .d
out, so it prints on all five backends,
.BR -tile s
if it has to, and opens in the editor when somebody wants to fix a
title by hand:
.DS
.ta 8n
	vellum -book amp?.d > contents.d
	vellum -ps contents.d amp?.d | lpr
.DE
The sheets stay FILES.
A book is a contents drawing plus a shell glob in the right order, not
a container format.

.SH "FRAGMENTS AND REVISIONS"
Neither costs any code, because both are files.
.PP
A FRAGMENT is a drawing.
A shop-standard detail \- a filter section, a title-block legend, a
north arrow, a revision cloud \- is a
.B .d
file in a fragments directory;
its index is
.BR ls .
.B "hrclip < /usr/vellum/frag/legend.d"
from any shell puts it on the PRIMARY selection, and a MIDDLE CLICK in
the editor pastes it at the pointer, because Paste has parsed
.B .d
text out of the selection since the beginning.
/usr/vellum/frag ships with a starter set; copy it, add your own,
delete ours.
.PP
A REVISION is a file.
When the frame stamp's
.B $R
says B,
.B "cp amp1.d amp1.d.B"
is the whole ceremony, and the record is the directory \- no SCCS, no
hidden state, no format change.
The project.mk skeleton grows the two targets that make it a workflow:
.B rev:
copies every sheet to its
.BI . REV
twin, and
.B drift:
runs
.B "vellum -diff"
on each sheet against that twin, so its exit status gates
.B print:
and nothing unreviewed reaches paper.
.B review:
hangs the marked-up drawing on the wall instead.
The gallery ships the pair: /usr/vellum/eg/amp.d.A beside amp.d.
.SH "FILE FORMAT"
Drawings are plain text, one object per line, in grid units; unknown
lines are skipped, so old readers accept new files:
.DS
.ta 8n
	vellum1
	U 5 mm
	Y NPN 20 30 0 0 Q1 -
	W 10 10 20 10
	L 5 5 9 9 /1.0
	T 12 4 s1 Power supply
	S box 60 10 75 20 Fetch
	K harrow 67 20 67 24 @67,20 -
	P 3 5 50 10 55 5 60
	A 30 50 4 0 180
	N 17 10 OUT
	D 10 40 24 40
	G 2
.DE
.B Y
symbol,
.B W
wire,
.B L
line,
.B B
box,
.B C
circle,
.B T
text (with an
.BI s n
size),
.B S
a stretchable shape,
.B K
a connector whose ends are `\-' (free) or
.BI @ x,y
(the attachment's grid point, re-resolved on load),
.B P
a polyline,
.B A
an arc (centre, radius, angles),
.B N
a net name,
.B D
a dimension (empty text = the automatic label),
.B U
the sheet-units header and
.B G
a group header covering the following n objects.
An optional
.BI / flags . layer
token carries line style, fill, bold, hatch, smooth, vertical text
and the layer.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 4n 30n
	/usr/vellum/sym/*.sym	the symbol libraries
	/usr/vellum/lib/*	the helper BINARIES the editor execs: the
		headless exporters (velxport), the dialogs
		(veldlg), the print preview (velprev) and
		the palette painter (velpal).  Stencil
		libraries live in sym/, not here \- they are
		not the same kind of thing
	/usr/vellum/etc/libs	which of them load at start-up
	/usr/vellum/etc/symbols	the user scratch library
	/usr/vellum/etc/frame.d	the Frame stamp's template
	/usr/vellum/etc/project.mk	the sheet-set Makefile skeleton:
		cp it next to the sheets and set SHEETS \-
		check: and drift: gate print:, ps: and
		bom:, and rev:/review: run the revision
		workflow
	/usr/vellum/etc/library.mk	the stencil-LIBRARY Makefile
		skeleton: a library is a build product, its
		sources are ordinary drawings, and
		-symcheck gates it the way -check gates a
		print
	/usr/vellum/frag/*.d	the FRAGMENT starter set: a revision
		legend, a north arrow, a revision cloud,
		a graphic scale bar, a tolerance note.  A
		fragment is a drawing and the library is
		a directory: hrclip one onto the
		selection and middle-click it into place
	/usr/vellum/eg/*.d	SAMPLE drawings, one per domain:
		amp logic (schematics), flow lan struct
		(diagrams), bracket (mechanical, with
		dimensions), feed oneline office (P&ID,
		one-line, floor plan), psu1 psu2 (a two-
		sheet set for -net merging), curve
		(a velgraph plot, annotated), plate
		(imported from plate.dxf, kept beside
		it), panel (a sheet-metal plate with
		cut-outs, for the takeoff).  amp.d.A is
		amp.d's approved REVISION, kept beside
		it: try vellum -diff amp.d.A amp.d.
		Open one, or feed them to the exporters,
		to see every feature working.
	/usr/vellum/eg/sk/*.d	the STENCIL SKETCHES: the nine ordinary
		drawings that build /usr/vellum/sym/pid.sym
		through -mksym, driven by library.mk.  They
		rebuild that library byte for byte, which is
		how the converter proves itself
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B symedit ,
.B veldxf ,
.B velgraph ,
.B zview ,
.B zman ,
.B lpr
.SH NOTES
A
.B "-diff -mark"
markup holds the new revision PLUS the removals, so a markup of two
crowded sheets can carry more than the 400 objects a drawing holds
when it is opened again; it always prints.
.PP
Wires connect only through junction dots; connectors never dot.
One level of undo only; the printed page and the pic figure are the
first-class outputs.
